(TIM-46 - a timer that was never started answers every property with
 zero and reports itself not running, and stopping it before it has ever
 been started is not an error. What it is instead is nonsense: with no
 start recorded, the stop leaves a duration equal to the whole reading of
 the clock the timer measures with - about 22 hours on the machine this
 was run on, which is that machine's uptime, not any interval the program
 asked for. Printed as a verdict rather than a number because the number
 is different on every machine and every boot.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox TIM-46.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   0
   0
   0
   0
   0
   an unstarted timer is not running
   stopping an unstarted timer is harmless
   and leaves a duration far longer than the program ran
)
a timer called 'never started'.
print 'never started''s start time.
print 'never started''s end time.
print 'never started''s duration in seconds.
print 'never started''s duration in milliseconds.
print 'never started''s elapsed in milliseconds.
If 'never started''s running then, print "an unstarted timer says it is running".
Otherwise, print "an unstarted timer is not running".
Stop the 'never started'.
If 'never started''s running then, print "stopping an unstarted timer left it running".
Otherwise, print "stopping an unstarted timer is harmless".
If 'never started''s duration in milliseconds is greater than 1000 then, print "and leaves a duration far longer than the program ran".
Otherwise, print "and leaves a duration of {'never started''s duration in milliseconds} milliseconds".
